comments.body:
@Navid200 If you can confirm that the error recieved after the warmup is a calibration error, then I can confirm this is not an xDrip error. Therefore this issue can be closed because there is nothing that can be done in the xDrip code.

Unless there was a way to overcome the error, but for that we need more information from users. For myself I can confirm that I was able to overcome the error and successfully get readings after a no code restart. I took about 1-2 hours after the warmup but it finally accepts the calibration values and then starts to work. I think what finally did it was to enter 3 glucose values at the same time with all the same value. You need to check the transmitter queue in the status page that shows 3 values in the queue before it makes the next connection. I'm not sure if it was just random luck or not so more testing is needed. 

Perhaps better user error codes are needed. The calibration error code in xDrip is too genetic. When I got the code I stopped the xDrip app and started the Dexcom app. It connected and then showed me a more clear message. Something like calibration rejected please wait until xxxx time to try again (which was 15 mins in the future). Even trying to calibrate with the Dexcom app would continue to result in an error and telling me to wait. However after an hour I tried to calibrate again and that time it worked.

@nickb24 If the problem is using no-code after a restart only, using no-code with a new sensor should work fine with no errors.  

In 7 days, I will restart using 0000 and report what error I get.
5 to 10 days after that, I will start a new sensor with 0000 and report if I get any errors.

@Navid200 I've been running my own version with this PR applied: https://github.com/NightscoutFoundation/xDrip/pull/1551
I've been running it for 2 months now with no issues and my logs don't have the error message. 

If you run the app with the extra debug logs, that would help in diagnosing the issue if it happens again. You can turn it off after.
